Theoretic Time Complexities:
Bubble Sort
Array Size | Best TC  |  Worst TC | Best Time | Worst Time
    100		O(n)	O(n^2)		99	10000
    10000	O(n)	O(n^2)		9999	100000000
    1000000	O(n)	O(n^2)		999999	1000000000000
Insertion Sort
Array Size | Best TC  |  Worst TC | Best Time | Worst Time
    100		O(n)	O(n^2)		99	10000
    10000	O(n)	O(n^2)		9999	100000000
    1000000	O(n)	O(n^2)		999999	1000000000000
Selection Sort
Array Size | Best TC  |  Worst TC | Best Time | Worst Time
    100		O(n^2)	  O(n^2)	10e4	10000
    10000	O(n^2)	  O(n^2)	10e8	100000000
    1000000	O(n^2)	  O(n^2)	10e12	1000000000000

Absolute Timing Scale:
The timing scale for the runtime matches well with the time complexities for 
bubble, insertion, and selection sort. For instance, the best runtime for array
size 100 for bubble sort (already sorted) took 99 swaps; in other words, O(n).
With that being said, the data that came from the runtime replicates the theoretic
time complexities.

Algorithm Analysis:
Each respective algorithm performed as expected except the selection sort seemed to
out-perform the insertion sort. The smaller amount of data gathered could be
the cause of this. From the data, Bubble sort performs terribly while
Selection sort and Insertion performed better and similarly.
As for the sorting process; the already sorted array took the minimum
amount of time to sort for every array size along with the reversed
array formation. For the most part, this made sense, since an already sorted
array should take only O(n) or n-1 turns to sort it. According to the
results, the best case for the data was approximately O(n) for 
each case. As for the worst case for all but the selection sort was: O(n^2).

Code improvements:
I could have used actual pointers (*) for the arrays so I could dynamically modify
the amount of elements and delete the allocated data once finished with the sorting
process. This would likely prevent the segmentation default at the end of the
program. Another thing I could have done was to make a for-each loop to loop the
different sorts; making the code more efficient.